Z||000000||Philphine||06-08-2001||11:23 PM||||dose it do anything for headlights that come up so far then flop back down, or is it for repair of another problem? this is an '87. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||WampusCat||06-08-2001||11:46 PM||||That's what mine were doing when the bushings were crumbling.  The kit from RD fixed it.  Might want to take one apart and look inside just to make sure.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dwayne Mohr||06-09-2001||06:51 AM||||Definitly sounds like a stripped or crumbling cog.  Rodney's kit should fix it.  Easy to take apart. just drill out the rivets. you can replace them with small sheet metal screws. About #8 or 10. Need some silicon to form a new seal around the edge. Be sure to clean out all the coagulated GM grease and replace it with some molybased grease.||||reg||12
Z||000003||mblackwell||06-09-2001||09:29 AM||||I thought 87s and 88s had no rivets to drill out.||||reg||1
Z||000004||kslish||06-09-2001||06:38 PM||||Philphine, your problem is the classic failed bushing problem.  I just did my '87 GT and you can take the motor apart with a socket set.  It's the '84-'86 motors that are riveted together.  I've never seen an '87-'88 motor that the gear itself has gone bad in...it's always been the bushings.  <P>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||ThatRickGuy||06-09-2001||07:31 PM||||Went threw this over the summer. it's not too hard to fix, basic tools, just pull the entire head light unit out, the mounting thing and all, it just makes it easier to get the motor/gear out. <P>The design is pretty ingenious, pre-87 can be a hassle to replace the gear, so the changed the set up, the gear isn't attached to the shaft, instead it has like a 3 leaf clover cut out arround the shaft. The shaft as 3 bump that line up w/ the peeks on the gear clover. those 3 little openings are the important part. there are 3 plastic cylinders that go in there, once they get old and start cracking, little bits will still catch and raise the light a bit, but the spring will knock it back down.<P>so in short, pull it out, open the case (3 small metric bolts) clean out all the old plastic dust, and insert the 3 new pieces from Rodney's set. really simple once it's open in front of ya.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||06-11-2001||03:04 PM||||That's EXACTLY what my passenger-side headlight was doing (88 Coupe) and Rodney's kit fixed them up good as new.  It's a pretty easy job, too, provided you read the directions well (which I didn't...there's a lip on the passenger-side gear you need to file down in order to reassemble the motor and I tried for half and hour to put it together without filing before I gave up and re-read the directions!).||||reg||14
